{
  "title": "∑ Sum of an AP — Gauss's Brilliant Trick",
  "hook": "Legend says young Carl Friedrich Gauss amazed his teacher by instantly summing 1+2+3+...+100 = 5050. His trick? Pair the first with the last: 1+100=101, 2+99=101, ... That's 50 pairs of 101 = 5050!",
  "explanation": "<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Sum Formula (using a and d)</div><strong>Sₙ = n/2 × [2a + (n−1)d]</strong></div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Sum Formula (using first and last term)</div><strong>Sₙ = n/2 × (a + l)</strong></div><p><b>Gauss's Trick Illustrated:</b> Sum of 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 100:</p><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Pairing</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Sum of pair</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1 + 100</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">101</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">2 + 99</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">101</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">3 + 98</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">101</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">...</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">...</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">50 + 51</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">101</td></tr></table><div style=\"background:#F5F5F5;border-left:3px solid #4A90D9;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;margin:6px 0;font-size:0.9rem\">50 pairs × 101 = 5050. In general: n/2 × (first + last).</div><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> To find the nth term from partial sums: <b>aₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁</b> (for n ≥ 2).</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> Find the sum of first 20 terms of the AP: 3, 7, 11, 15, ...<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Identify a, d, n</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">a = 3, d = 4, n = 20</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Use Sₙ = n/2 × [2a + (n−1)d]</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">S₂₀ = 20/2 × [6 + 19×4] = 10 × [6 + 76] = 10 × 82 = 820</span></div></div></div><br><b>Alternative:</b> Find last term first: a₂₀ = 3 + 19(4) = 79. S₂₀ = 20/2 × (3 + 79) = 10 × 82 = 820. Same answer!",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Find the sum of first 15 terms of the AP: 10, 7, 4, 1, ...",
    "hint": "a = 10, d = −3, n = 15.",
    "answer": "S₁₅ = 15/2 × [2(10) + 14(−3)] = 15/2 × [20 − 42] = 15/2 × (−22) = −165."
  },
  "fun_fact": "Gauss reportedly did this calculation at age 7! His teacher, J.G. Büttner, was so impressed that he got the young Gauss special math books to study."
